    text: `Education provides the foundation for individual development and plays an important role in preparing people for employment and responsible participation in society. In a changing economy, formal education needs to be supported by practical skills, digital knowledge, communication abilities, and opportunities for continuous learning. Skill development programmes can help young people and workers acquire competencies that are relevant to different sectors of the economy. Such programmes may include technical training, vocational education, computer skills, communication training, and practical experience. The effectiveness of skill development depends on the quality of training and its connection with actual employment opportunities. Training institutions need suitable equipment, qualified instructors, updated learning materials, and practical facilities. Courses should be reviewed regularly so that students learn skills that are relevant to changing technologies and workplace requirements. Industry participation can also help training institutions understand the skills required by employers and provide learners with opportunities for internships, apprenticeships, and practical exposure. Apprenticeship programmes are useful because they combine classroom learning with workplace experience. A trainee can observe professional practices, learn how equipment and systems are used, and understand the importance of punctuality, teamwork, safety, and communication. Employers can also identify potential workers who have developed relevant skills through practical training. Digital technology has created new opportunities for education and employment. Online learning platforms can provide access to courses, lectures, study materials, and assessments from different locations. Digital skills are increasingly useful in areas such as administration, banking, healthcare, education, commerce, manufacturing, and communication. However, access to technology is not equal for everyone. Students and workers in areas with limited internet connectivity or inadequate digital infrastructure may require additional support. Employment generation also depends on entrepreneurship and the development of small businesses. Training in financial management, marketing, digital payments, customer service, and business planning can help individuals understand the basic requirements of starting and managing an enterprise. Self-employment can create income for individuals and may also generate employment for others. Women and disadvantaged groups may require targeted opportunities and support to participate fully in skill development and employment programmes. Career guidance is another important element of education and employment planning. Students often need information about different occupations, educational qualifications, entrance requirements, training opportunities, and career pathways. Reliable guidance can help learners make informed choices according to their interests, abilities, and available opportunities. Schools, colleges, training institutions, employers, and public agencies can contribute to career awareness. Employment services can also support job seekers by providing information about vacancies, recruitment processes, skill requirements, and training opportunities. Continuous learning has become increasingly important because technology and workplace practices change over time. A person may need to update existing skills or learn new ones during different stages of a career. Therefore, education should encourage curiosity, problem solving, adaptability, and a willingness to learn. The broader objective of skill development is not simply to provide certificates but to improve practical competence and employability. When education is combined with quality training, industry exposure, career guidance, entrepreneurship support, and opportunities for lifelong learning, individuals can become better prepared for changing employment conditions and contribute more effectively to economic and social development.`
