    text: `Public healthcare programmes play an important role in improving access to health services and reducing the financial burden associated with illness. Government health initiatives generally include measures related to prevention, early detection, treatment, maternal and child health, nutrition, immunisation, disease control, and health awareness. The effectiveness of such programmes depends on appropriate planning, trained healthcare workers, adequate infrastructure, reliable medicines, accurate records, and cooperation from communities. Preventive healthcare aims to reduce the risk of disease before serious illness develops. Regular health check ups, vaccination, sanitation, safe drinking water, balanced nutrition, physical activity, and awareness about healthy habits can contribute to better health outcomes. Early detection is also important because some diseases can be managed more effectively when identified at an early stage. Screening and health awareness activities can encourage people to seek professional advice when symptoms or risk factors are identified. Primary healthcare facilities are often the first point of contact between citizens and the health system. These facilities may provide basic treatment, maternal and child health services, immunisation, health education, disease prevention, and referrals to higher centres when specialised care is required. Strong primary healthcare can reduce unnecessary pressure on larger hospitals and improve access for people living in rural and remote areas. Maternal and child health requires continued attention because health during pregnancy, childbirth, and early childhood can influence long term development. Pregnant women may require regular check ups, nutritional support, counselling, and access to skilled healthcare services. Children need timely immunisation, adequate nutrition, growth monitoring, and appropriate healthcare. Families and communities also play an important role in recognising health risks and using available services. Communicable disease control requires coordination between healthcare institutions, local authorities, laboratories, and communities. Measures may include surveillance, testing, treatment, vaccination where applicable, sanitation, vector control, and public awareness. Accurate information helps health authorities identify patterns and respond to outbreaks. Individuals can contribute by following preventive guidance and seeking medical assistance when required. Non communicable diseases such as diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular conditions, and certain cancers are also important public health concerns. Lifestyle factors, age, family history, and other conditions can influence the risk of such diseases. Regular physical activity, balanced diets, avoidance of harmful substances, and timely medical consultation can support prevention and management. Health programmes may use awareness campaigns and screening activities to encourage early action. Digital technology can support public healthcare through electronic records, online appointments, telemedicine, data reporting, and communication systems. Digital tools can improve the availability of information and help health authorities monitor services, but they require reliable infrastructure, trained personnel, data security, and appropriate safeguards for personal information. Health awareness is most effective when information is simple, accurate, and suitable for the people receiving it. Government agencies, schools, healthcare workers, local organisations, and community representatives can work together to promote healthy practices. People should also be encouraged to distinguish reliable medical information from unverified claims circulating through social media or other informal sources. The broader purpose of preventive healthcare is to reduce avoidable illness, encourage early action, and improve the overall health of communities. Effective public health programmes require accessible services, trained personnel, reliable infrastructure, accurate information, community participation, and regular evaluation. When preventive care is combined with timely treatment and responsible health behaviour, healthcare systems can respond more effectively to both immediate health needs and long term public health challenges.`
