    text: "The High Court of Judicature at Patna considered a criminal miscellaneous application filed by Kumar Anuj, son of Late Ramjee Prasad, arising out of Complaint Case No. 799(C) of 2011 of Begusarai. The petitioner sought quashing of the order dated 24 May 2013 passed by the learned Chief Judicial Magistrate, Begusarai. The matter was heard by the High Court after notice had been validly served upon opposite party no. 2, who did not appear. The petitioner was represented by his learned counsel, while the State was represented by the learned Additional Public Prosecutor. According to the complaint petition, the alleged occurrence took place on 6 May 2011 at about 8:30 in the morning during an election for the posts of Mukhiya and Panchayat Samiti. The complainant was working as a polling agent at Booth No. 315 and was also a voter at Booth No. 316. It was alleged that the petitioner suddenly slapped and abused the complainant. The police constables present at the polling station allegedly handed the petitioner over to the police, and he was detained at Mufassil Police Station until about 5:00 in the evening. The complainant further alleged that his signature was obtained on a blank paper during the detention. It was also alleged that the assault caused injury to the complainant's recently operated left eye, for which he received treatment at Sadar Hospital, Begusarai. The complainant stated that because of the alleged assault and detention, he was prevented from casting his vote. He further alleged that the petitioner had misused his official position and authority. The principal submission on behalf of the petitioner concerned the protection available to public servants under Section 197 of the Code of Criminal Procedure. It was submitted that the petitioner was a public servant within the meaning of Section 21 of the Indian Penal Code and was working as a Sub-Divisional Officer at the relevant time. According to the petitioner, prosecution could not proceed without prior sanction from the competent authority. In the absence of such sanction, it was argued that the prosecution and the order under challenge were legally unsustainable. In support of this contention, reliance was placed upon the decision of the Hon'ble Supreme Court in G.C. Manjunath and Others v. Seetaram, reported as (2025) 5 SCC 390. The Supreme Court had considered the protection available to public servants in cases involving alleged excesses committed while discharging official duties. It emphasized that public servants perform functions of the State and often exercise discretion in circumstances requiring timely action. The protection under Section 197 exists so that public servants may perform their duties fearlessly and without constant apprehension of legal proceedings, particularly when they act in good faith. The Supreme Court also clarified that the protection is not unlimited. Every offence committed by a public servant does not automatically receive the benefit of Section 197. The alleged act must have a reasonable connection with the official duties of the public servant. The protection cannot be used where official position is merely employed as an excuse for committing wrongful acts. However, even when an officer exceeds official powers or acts improperly, prior sanction may still be necessary if there is a reasonable connection between the act complained of and the official duty. The High Court therefore examined whether the allegations against the petitioner had a reasonable nexus with his official functions. At the relevant time, the petitioner was admittedly serving as a Sub-Divisional Officer and was performing duties connected with the conduct of the election. The alleged occurrence took place at a polling station during the election process, and the allegation concerning the complainant's detention at the police station also related to the exercise of official authority during that period. After considering the rival submissions and the material on record, the High Court held that there existed a reasonable connection between the acts complained of and the official duties of the petitioner. Consequently, previous sanction under Section 197 of the Code of Criminal Procedure was a condition precedent for the Court to take cognizance of the alleged offences. Since no such sanction had been obtained, the impugned order could not be sustained. Accordingly, the application was allowed, and the order dated 24 May 2013 passed by the Chief Judicial Magistrate, Begusarai in Complaint Case No. 799(C) of 2011 was quashed."
