    wordCount: 774,
    text: "The High Court of Jharkhand at Ranchi considered a Civil Review Petition filed by Manju Devi and Manoj Kumar against Central Coalfields Limited and its concerned authorities. The review petition arose out of the judgment dated 22 November 2023 passed in L.P.A. No. 403 of 2023, by which the appeal filed by the petitioners had been dismissed. The petitioners contended that the earlier judgment had not considered a decision concerning a similarly situated person and requested a lenient approach on the issue of limitation in cases relating to compassionate appointment. The dispute concerned the claim of Manoj Kumar for compassionate appointment following the death of his father, Haripat Saw, who was a permanent employee of Central Coalfields Limited and had been working as a Trammer at Saunda Colliery. He died in harness on 10 August 2009. Manju Devi was his wife and Manoj Kumar was his son. A representation seeking compassionate appointment was submitted on 11 June 2011, approximately twenty-two months after the death of the employee. The application was initially rejected on 12 December 2012 on the ground that it had been filed beyond the prescribed period. On 20 November 2017, the competent authority passed a speaking order and again rejected the claim. The authority noted that the limitation period for making an application for compassionate appointment had been extended to one and a half years by a circular dated 3 November 2009. The application, however, had been submitted after one year and ten months from the date of death and was therefore beyond the prescribed period. The authority also relied upon a discrepancy concerning the date of birth and age of Manoj Kumar. The record showed that in Form PS-3, the deceased employee had declared that his son was eight years old as on 20 September 2002. On the other hand, Manoj Kumar had disclosed his date of birth as 15 August 1987. The authority considered this discrepancy significant and found his candidature doubtful and suspicious. It was also noted that if the declaration in Form PS-3 were accepted, Manoj Kumar would have been a minor at the relevant time, yet no request had been made to include his name in the live roster. The petitioners did not provide a satisfactory explanation for these circumstances. The petitioners thereafter filed another writ petition, which was dismissed on merits on 1 May 2023. The Court considered three principal grounds: limitation, the discrepancy in the date of birth, and the substantial period that had elapsed since the death of the employee. The subsequent Letters Patent Appeal was also dismissed on 22 November 2023. The petitioners then approached the High Court by way of the present review petition. While considering the scope of review, the Court referred to several judgments of the Supreme Court. It reiterated that the power of review is not equivalent to appellate power and cannot be used as an appeal in disguise. A review may be exercised where a new and important matter or evidence, despite due diligence, was not within the knowledge of the party or could not be produced earlier. It may also be exercised where there is a mistake or error apparent on the face of the record. However, an error requiring a long process of reasoning cannot ordinarily be treated as an apparent error. The Court further observed that a judicial decision is an authority for what it actually decides. The principle upon which a case is decided, known as the ratio decidendi, is of particular importance. Precedents must be applied after examining the facts of the case in which they are relied upon. The judgment cited by the petitioners concerning a lenient approach to delay in compassionate appointment was found distinguishable because the facts of that case were materially different. In the present matter, the claim had not been rejected solely because of delay. The competent authority had given several reasons, including the discrepancy in the age and date of birth of Manoj Kumar, the absence of any request for inclusion in the live roster, and the failure to provide an acceptable explanation for the delay. The Court found that these findings were based on the materials available on record and could not be described as perverse or contrary to law. The Court also emphasized that compassionate appointment is intended to provide immediate financial assistance to a family facing the crisis caused by the death of its earning member. A claim made after a considerable lapse of time may defeat the very purpose of such appointment. Since the petitioners had failed to establish any ground warranting review of the earlier judgment, the Court dismissed the review petition."
