    text: "The High Court of Judicature at Bombay considered Bail Application No. 3449 of 2026 filed by Arif Ajij Kureshi against the State of Maharashtra. The application was made under Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, seeking bail in connection with C.R. No. 398 of 2022 registered with Manchar Police Station, Pune. The applicant was charged under Section 302 read with Section 34 of the Indian Penal Code, 1860. The matter was heard by Justice Milind N. Jadhav on September 9, 2026. The applicant had been in custody for more than three years and ten months after his arrest on October 20, 2022. He was accused of committing murder along with two other co-accused persons. The incident had occurred during the intervening night of October 16 and 17, 2022. There was no direct eyewitness to the actual incident and the prosecution case was based on circumstantial evidence. One witness had seen a quarrel between the victim and the three accused persons earlier in the evening. According to the witness, the applicant and another accused had warned the victim not to argue with them. The same witness later stated that he saw the applicant and the other accused persons still quarrelling at midnight. Apart from this statement, the applicant was alleged to have inflicted fist and kick blows. The prosecution opposed the Bail Application. However, the Court noted that the applicant had already remained incarcerated for more than three years and ten months. Although the trial had commenced, its progress was extremely slow. The Court found no realistic possibility of the trial being completed in the near future or foreseeable future. Therefore, considering prolonged incarceration and the agony caused by the continuing trial, the Court was inclined to grant bail. The Court explained that while deciding a Bail Application, the principal rule is to ascertain whether the accused is likely to appear before the Court for trial. Other important factors include the gravity of the offence, the likelihood of the accused repeating the offence while on bail, the possibility of influencing witnesses or tampering with evidence, and the antecedents of the accused. The Court also observed that the material available for consideration in a bail proceeding is limited. At the same time, prolonged trials and overcrowding in prisons create serious concerns concerning the liberty of under-trial prisoners. The judgment emphasized Article 21 of the Constitution, which protects the rights to life and personal liberty. An under-trial who remains in custody for a very long period pending trial has a right to speedy justice. The High Court's power to grant bail in cases involving prolonged incarceration is wide, and the settled principle is that bail is the rule and refusal is the exception. The judgment referred to several decisions of the Supreme Court and High Courts, including Satender Kumar Antil, Gudikanti Narasimhulu, Maneka Gandhi, Hussainara Khatoon, Sanjay Chandra, and Union of India v. K. A. Najeeb. The Court also discussed the presumption of innocence. A person accused of an offence is presumed innocent until the prosecution proves guilt before the Court. The purpose of bail is primarily to secure the attendance of the accused at trial. Bail is neither punitive nor preventative in its ordinary purpose. Detention before conviction should not become a form of punishment. Judicial discretion in granting bail must be exercised carefully and according to the facts and circumstances of each case. The right to speedy trial was considered in detail. The judgment referred to Abdul Rehman Antulay v. R.S. Nayak, where the Supreme Court explained that the right to speedy trial flows from Article 21 and covers investigation, inquiry, trial, appeal, revision and retrial. From the perspective of an accused, the period of remand and pre-conviction detention should be as short as possible. Undue delay may cause worry, anxiety, expense and disturbance to the accused. It may also impair the ability to defend the case if witnesses die, disappear or become unavailable. The Court referred to earlier cases in which bail had been granted because of long incarceration and the unlikelihood of the trial being completed within the foreseeable future. The judgment also considered the principle that constitutional courts can release under-trial prisoners on bail when continued incarceration would seriously affect their fundamental rights. The Court concluded that the present applicant, having remained in custody for more than three years and ten months with no distinct possibility of the trial commencing or concluding in the near future, had made out a case for bail. The Bail Application was allowed subject to conditions. The applicant was directed to be released from prison and to furnish a personal recognizance bond of Rs. 25,000 with one or two sureties in the like amount. He was required to report to the Investigating Officer at Manchar Police Station once every month on the first Sunday between 10:00 a.m. and 12:00 p.m. He had to cooperate with the trial and attend the Trial Court on all dates unless specifically exempted. He was not to take unnecessary adjournments. The applicant was also prohibited from leaving the State of Maharashtra without prior permission of the Trial Court. He was directed not to influence witnesses or tamper with evidence in any manner. He had to keep the Investigating Officer informed of his current residential address and mobile contact number and report any change in those details. The Court clarified that its observations were prima facie and would not affect the merits of the trial. Any violation of the conditions could result in cancellation of bail."
