    text: "The Court considered an application for anticipatory bail filed by the applicant in connection with a case registered at the concerned police station. The applicant stated that he had reason to believe that he might be arrested in relation to the allegations mentioned in the First Information Report. He therefore requested protection from arrest during the investigation. Learned counsel for the applicant submitted that the applicant was innocent and had been falsely implicated in the case because of a dispute between the parties. It was argued that the allegations did not disclose any serious role of the applicant and that the applicant had not caused any injury to the complainant. Counsel further submitted that the applicant was a permanent resident of the area, had a fixed address, and was ready to cooperate with the investigation. The prosecution opposed the application and submitted that the allegations required proper investigation. The learned Public Prosecutor argued that the applicant should not be granted protection from arrest at this stage because certain facts were yet to be verified. It was submitted that the investigating officer needed to examine the statements of the complainant and other witnesses and collect relevant documents. The Court heard both sides and examined the material placed before it. The Court observed that an application for anticipatory bail has to be considered on the facts and circumstances of each case. At this stage, the Court was not required to conduct a detailed examination of the evidence or give a final finding regarding the guilt of the applicant. The Court was required to consider the nature of the accusation, the role attributed to the applicant, the possibility of his cooperating with the investigation, and the possibility of his influencing witnesses or interfering with evidence. In the present case, the Court noted that the applicant had expressed his willingness to appear before the investigating officer whenever required. The material placed before the Court did not show that custodial interrogation of the applicant was necessary for the investigation. The Court therefore considered it appropriate to grant limited protection from arrest, subject to conditions. The Court also directed the applicant to remain available to the investigating agency and to respond to lawful notices issued during the investigation. Accordingly, in the event of arrest, the applicant was directed to be released on bail on furnishing a personal bond of Rs. 25,000 with one surety of the like amount to the satisfaction of the arresting officer or the concerned Trial Court. The applicant was directed to appear before the Investigating Officer on the dates and at the place communicated to him. The applicant was directed not to threaten, influence, or contact the complainant or any prosecution witness. He was also directed not to tamper with evidence and not to commit any similar offence during the period of protection. He was further directed not to leave India without prior permission of the Court and to inform the Investigating Officer about any change in his residential address or mobile telephone number. The Court clarified that the protection granted by the order would remain subject to compliance with all the conditions. Any violation of the conditions would give the prosecution liberty to seek appropriate orders in accordance with law. The Court also clarified that the observations made while deciding the anticipatory bail application were only for considering the request for protection and would not affect the investigation or the trial. The application was accordingly disposed of with the above directions. The Court made it clear that the order would operate only in accordance with the conditions stated above."
