    text: "The High Court considered a writ petition filed by the petitioner challenging an administrative order passed by a government authority. The petitioner stated that he had submitted an application before the department seeking consideration of his request for appointment and that the application had remained pending for a considerable period. According to the petitioner, several reminders had been submitted to the concerned authority, but no final decision had been communicated to him. Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner was entitled to a fair and timely consideration of his representation. It was argued that the authority was under a duty to examine the documents submitted by the petitioner and to pass an appropriate order in accordance with the applicable rules. Counsel further submitted that the petitioner was not asking the Court to direct the authority to grant the final relief immediately, but was only seeking a direction for disposal of his pending representation within a reasonable period. The learned counsel appearing for the department opposed the petition and submitted that the representation was under consideration. It was stated that certain documents were required to be verified before a final decision could be taken. The department further submitted that the petitioner had not suffered any final rejection and therefore there was no cause for interference by the Court at that stage. The Court heard the submissions of both sides and examined the documents placed on record. The Court observed that an administrative authority is required to consider a representation made by a person in accordance with law and within a reasonable period. The Court further observed that a person approaching the Court cannot ordinarily seek a direction requiring the authority to decide a matter in a particular manner unless the law clearly provides for such relief. The Court can, however, direct the competent authority to consider and decide a pending representation when circumstances justify such a direction. In the present case, the Court noticed that the petitioner had submitted a representation along with relevant documents and that the representation had not yet been finally decided. The Court therefore considered it appropriate to dispose of the petition with a direction to the competent authority. The concerned authority was directed to examine the petitioner's representation and the documents available on record and to pass a reasoned and speaking order in accordance with law. The decision was directed to be taken within eight weeks from the date of receipt of a certified copy of the Court order. The Court clarified that it had not examined the merits of the petitioner's claim and had not expressed any opinion regarding his entitlement to the relief sought before the department. The competent authority was required to take an independent decision after considering the applicable rules, relevant documents, and all other facts placed before it. The Court further directed that the decision taken by the authority should be communicated to the petitioner without unnecessary delay. If the petitioner remained aggrieved by the decision, he would be at liberty to avail himself of such remedy as may be available to him in accordance with law. The Court also made it clear that the time granted for deciding the representation should not be treated as a direction to grant the relief claimed by the petitioner. The writ petition was accordingly disposed of with the above directions. There was no order as to costs. A copy of the order was directed to be supplied to the concerned authority for compliance. The Court expected the department to deal with the representation fairly and in accordance with the applicable legal and administrative requirements."
